From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 16:49:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] bytecode: swap it to the code store, and drop cold code under
 pressure
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Packed bytecode of functions that never ran is about a sixth of what a
compiled bundle keeps, and the executable bytes of functions that ran
once stay forever. Both can live in an embedder's code store.

At the idle round, compile roots (held weakly, so a collected script
costs nothing) are walked: a never-run block's packed form goes to the
store behind a 12-byte descriptor with a hash, and a function that
already ran gets a copy there. expand() fetches a swapped block back,
checks length and hash (registers are read unchecked, so other bytes
must never run), and keeps the copy. ks_swap_under_pressure, safe at an
arbitrary allocation since it touches no GC state, drops the executable
bytes of every such block no frame or reader pins; the next run fetches
them again. Frames pin their block in push_frame and unpin it in
pop_frame, through a count in the length word, saturating and sticky.

